# Laravel Conventions

Full detail in `docs/practices/database.md`, `docs/practices/architecture.md` and
`docs/practices/security.md`.

## Multi-tenancy

Tenant data hangs off `organization_id`. Every read and write is scoped to the acting
user's organization — controller, service, and job alike. Background jobs do not
inherit a session, so they must carry and re-apply the organization explicitly.

A missing scope looks exactly like working code until it leaks another tenant's data.
Treat an unscoped query on a tenant table as a bug, not a style issue.

## Structure

Controllers stay thin: validate, delegate, return. Business logic belongs in
`app/app/Services/`, data access in Eloquent models. This is a modular monolith —
do not split services out, and do not introduce a new architectural layer without a
measured reason.

Follow the patterns already in the directory you are editing rather than importing
conventions from elsewhere.

## Validation

Validate every input on the server, in a Form Request where there is more than a
couple of rules. Client-side validation is a UX affordance, never a control.

Prefer allowlists over denylists. Validate types and ranges, not just presence.

## Queries

Use Eloquent, which parameterizes automatically. Eager-load relations to avoid N+1 —
the common failure here is a Blade or Vue loop that triggers a query per row.

Do not add indexes, caches, or denormalized columns until profiling shows a real
bottleneck. Ship the clear query first.

## Migrations

Schema changes go in migrations, reversible where the operation allows. Keep data
migrations in their own files, separate from schema changes, so a rollback of one
does not silently undo the other.

## Errors and logging

Fail fast with clear, actionable messages. Log with enough context to debug, never
swallow an exception silently, and never expose an internal error to the client.

## Scan services and rules

Services and rules are contributed as JSON and fail quietly when wrong: a rule aimed
at data no `tasks.json` collects never matches, which is indistinguishable from a
compliant account. Run `php artisan scan:validate-rules` after any change to them.
